It looks like the poem text itself wasn't included in your message, so I can't annotate the *specific* lines yet. But I've built you a **complete, reusable annotated-reading template** with toggleable annotations and a glossary already defining "crispen," "suety," and "Arcturus." Just paste your poem's lines into the marked section (I've included example lines to show the pattern), and you're set.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<title>Annotated Reading</title>
<style>
  :root {
    --paper: #faf7f0;
    --ink: #23201c;
    --accent: #7a5c3e;
    --note-bg: #fff8e8;
  }
  body {
    font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
    background: var(--paper);
    color: var(--ink);
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2.5rem 1.5rem;
    line-height: 1.9;
  }
  h1 { font-size: 1.6rem; margin-bottom: 0.25rem; }
  .byline { color: var(--accent); font-style: italic; margin-top: 0; }

  .controls {
    margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;
    padding-bottom: 1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #ddd;
  }
  .toggle-btn {
    font-family: inherit;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    background: var(--accent);
    color: #fff;
    border: none;
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
    border-radius: 6px;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .toggle-btn:hover { opacity: 0.9; }

  .poem { font-size: 1.15rem; white-space: pre-wrap; }
  .line { display: block; }

  /* Annotated words */
  .term {
    border-bottom: 1px dashed var(--accent);
    cursor: help;
    position: relative;
  }
  /* When annotations are OFF, hide the visual cue */
  body.annotations-off .term { border-bottom: none; cursor: default; }

  /* Inline pop note */
  .note {
    display: none;
    background: var(--note-bg);
    border: 1px solid #e3d3ad;
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.85rem;
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-style: normal;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  body:not(.annotations-off) .term.open + .note { display: block; }
  .note .word { font-weight: bold; color: var(--accent); }

  /* Glossary */
  .glossary { margin-top: 3rem; border-top: 1px solid #ddd; padding-top: 1rem; }
  .glossary h2 { font-size: 1.2rem; }
  .glossary dt { font-weight: bold; color: var(--accent); margin-top: 0.75rem; }
  .glossary dd { margin: 0.15rem 0 0 0; }
</style>
</head>
<body class="annotations-off">

  <h1>Poem Title Here</h1>
  <p class="byline">by Author Name</p>

  <div class="controls">
    <button class="toggle-btn" id="toggle">Show annotations</button>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ PASTE YOUR POEM HERE ============
       Wrap any word you want defined in:
       <span class="term" data-note="Your note text.">word</span>
       Everything else is plain text. Example lines below. -->
  <div class="poem">
<span class="line">The morning light began to
<span class="term" data-note="crispen">crispen</span> at the sill,
</span>
<span class="line">a <span class="term" data-note="suety">suety</span> haze above the frozen hill,
</span>
<span class="line">while <span class="term" data-note="Arcturus">Arcturus</span> paled and slipped below the ridge.
</span>
  </div>
  <!-- ============ END POEM ============ -->

  <div class="glossary">
    <h2>Glossary</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt id="def-crispen">crispen <span style="font-weight:normal;font-style:italic;">(verb)</span></dt>
      <dd>To make or become crisp — to firm, curl, wrinkle, or sharpen at the edges. Often used of light, frost, leaves, or bread just as they turn brittle and defined.</dd>

      <dt id="def-suety">suety <span style="font-weight:normal;font-style:italic;">(adjective)</span></dt>
      <dd>Resembling <em>suet</em> (the hard, pale fat around the kidneys of cattle or sheep). Figuratively: thick, dense, pale, greasy, or heavy in texture — as of a fog, sky, or complexion.</dd>

      <dt id="def-arcturus">Arcturus <span style="font-weight:normal;font-style:italic;">(proper noun)</span></dt>
      <dd>The brightest star in the constellation Boötes and the fourth-brightest in the night sky. A reddish-orange giant whose name comes from Greek <em>Arktouros</em>, "guardian of the bear," because it appears to follow the Great Bear (Ursa Major).</dd>
    </dl>
  </div>

<script>
  const body = document.body;
  const btn = document.getElementById('toggle');

  // Master toggle for the whole page
  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    body.classList.toggle('annotations-off');
    const off = body.classList.contains('annotations-off');
    btn.textContent = off ? 'Show annotations' : 'Hide annotations';
    if (off) {
      // collapse any open individual notes
      document.querySelectorAll('.term.open').forEach(t => t.classList.remove('open'));
    }
  });

  // Build the inline note element after each term, and allow click-to-toggle
  document.querySelectorAll('.term').forEach(term => {
    const key = term.dataset.note.toLowerCase();
    const glossaryText = {
      crispen: "To make or become crisp — to firm, curl, or sharpen at the edges.",
      suety: "Resembling suet (hard pale animal fat); thick, dense, pale, or heavy.",
      arcturus: "The brightest star in Boötes; its name means 'guardian of the bear.'"
    };
    const note = document.createElement('span');
    note.className = 'note';
    note.innerHTML = '<span class="word">' + term.textContent + ':</span> ' +
                     (glossaryText[key] || term.dataset.note);
    term.after(note);

    // Individual click toggle (works when annotations are ON)
    term.addEventListener('click', () => {
      if (body.classList.contains('annotations-off')) return;
      term.classList.toggle('open');
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How it works
- **Master toggle** – The "Show annotations / Hide annotations" button turns all annotation cues on or off at once.
- **Per-word notes** – When annotations are on, dashed underlines mark annotated words; click any one to pop open its inline definition.
- **Glossary** – A permanent reference list at the bottom, already stocked with your three terms.

### To finish it
1. Replace the title, author, and the placeholder lines with your actual poem.
2. Wrap any word you want explained like this:
   `<span class="term" data-note="keyword">word</span>`
3. Add matching entries to the `glossaryText` object in the script and to the glossary list.

